1. The bin layout

Every standard command is its own real ELF32 binary: the sources live in
bin/ (cat.c, ls.c, cp.c, vi.c, ...) and the Makefile compiles each one
with plain host gcc -m32 (freestanding, no libc) into a standalone ELF32
binary in home/bin/ (<name>.o), embedded in the boot image (via home.tar)
as /bin/<name>.o. The kernel loads them at runtime through its in-kernel
ELF32 driver (kernel/elf.c), which resolves each program's symbols against
the kernel export table (include/tanja.h).
The shell runs a command by trying /bin/<name>.o first (so you can add or
override a standard command by dropping a file with the same name in /bin
at runtime), then /root/Programs or /home/<username/Programs for user-added programs.

2. Programming in TanjaOS

TinyCC ships as /bin/tcc.o, so 'tcc' is just another command. It compiles
real C99/C11 (the full tcc frontend) into ELF32 objects the kernel ELF
driver can run. The OS ships a real, standard C header set at /usr/include
- stdio.h, stdlib.h, string.h, unistd.h, ctype.h, time.h, math.h, errno.h,
assert.h, setjmp.h, signal.h, fcntl.h, inttypes.h, limits.h, locale.h,
fenv.h, iso646.h, stdatomic.h, stdalign.h, stdnoreturn.h, tgmath.h,
sys/... - and NOTHING custom: no tanja.h. The kernel API (console, printf,
keyboard, the fs_* filesystem functions, environment, timers) is declared
inside those standard headers, so a TanjaOS program is just a normal C
file.

tcc is strict by default, on purpose:

  * ALL warnings are hard compile-time errors (-Werror is injected
    first in the argument list).  Pass -Wno-error to get normal
    warning behavior back.  (#warning directives are exempt, like
    a real gcc -Werror build.)
  * After a clean compile the wrapper verifies EVERY external symbol
    the produced object references against the live kernel export
    table (tanja_export_count()/tanja_export_name() in kernel/elf.c).
    A program that calls something the kernel does not export fails
    AT COMPILE TIME with "undefined symbol" instead of failing to
    load at run time.  Weak undefined symbols are exempt (they bind
    to 0, per the C standard).
  * C11 u8"..." string literals work (parsed as UTF-8 narrow
    strings).  u/U wide literals are a clear compile-time error
    (there is no wchar/char16_t machinery).

    #include <stdio.h>

    void main(char* args) {
        printf("hi %s\n", args);
        return 0;
    }

Edit it with 'vi hello.c', compile and run it:

    tcc -c hello.c -o hello.o      # compile
    ./hello.o some args            # run

There is no linker, so 'tcc hello.c -o hello' (link an executable)
is not supported; the wrapper downgrades it to compile-only, names
the result hello.o and prints how to run it.

Logins start in the user's home directory, /home/home (shown as ~ in
the prompt; bare `cd` goes there too).

To run any program, just type its name: './hello.o', 'hello.o' or
'dir/prog.o' all work (there is no exec command - if a token isn't a
builtin or a /bin command but names an existing file, the shell runs
it: ELF32 objects as programs, text files as line-per-command scripts).

Programs are entered as void main(char* args) (the single command-line
argument string) - int main(void) works too. The kernel exports the full
stdio/stdlib/string/ctype/time/math surface (printf/scanf families,
malloc/calloc/realloc/free, FILE streams incl. fopen/fread/fwrite/fclose,
open/close/read/write/lseek, stat/fstat, qsort/bsearch, strtol family,
the x87 math library in all three tiers, locale/fenv/iso646 support,
64-bit division/modulo helpers __divdi3 and friends), so ordinary C
programs compile and run as-is. Anything NOT exported is caught at
compile time by the wrapper's symbol check (see above).

3. Building

Use 'make' to compile (this compiles the source, all bin/ commands, and
TinyCC - nothing else). Once compiling is finished, a bootable OS
image 'tanja-base' will be located at arch/x86/boot, boot it with:

    qemu-system-i386 -m 64 -kernel arch/x86/boot/tanja-base
 ___________________
[ REQUIRED PACKAGES ]
|-------------------|
| 1) make           |
| 2) nasm           |
| 3) gcc            |
|___________________|

For compiling on BSD systems, you should use gmake instead of make,
this is because BSD's make can't use the traditional linker.ld for TanjaOS.
You will see a file called "Makefile.bsd", this is the Makefile for BSD systems.
To use Makefile.bsd, use 'gmake <option> -f Makefile.bsd'.
 _________________________
[ REQUIRED PACKAGES (BSD) ]
|-------------------------|
| 1) gmake                |
| 2) nasm                 |
| 3) gcc                  |
|_________________________|
